WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:03.980
Chris: When working in ‘Agile’
development you will constantly hear the

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:10.339
terms ‘backlog’, ‘Epics’, ‘Features’,
‘Themes’ and ‘User Stories’, so

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:12.250
what do these different terms mean?

00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:15.199
Leah: Let’s first take a look at backlogs.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:19.570
There are two types of backlogs,
product backlogs and sprint backlogs.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.370
Chris: Product backlogs contain
the list of work items that need

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:24.910
to be completed for the project.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:29.590
Items are ranked in order of
importance, so that the team knows

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.939
which items need to be completed first.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:37.350
Simply put, the ‘product backlog’ is
like a ‘To-Do list’ of tasks, that

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:41.460
helps to drive progress and keeps the
development always moving forward.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.960
When there is capacity, members of
the team move items from the ‘product

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.040
backlog’ into the ‘sprint backlog’.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.100
A good thing to note is that the
‘product backlog’ is not set in stone,

00:00:52.549 --> 00:00:56.470
when working in ‘Agile’, projects are
constantly evolving and the ‘product

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.750
backlog’ is changed to reflect this.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.459
Leah: So, what about the sprint backlog?

00:01:01.849 --> 00:01:04.369
The ‘sprint backlog’ is where
the items taken from the

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.029
‘product backlog’ are placed.

00:01:06.509 --> 00:01:10.979
Once in the ‘sprint backlog’, the items
are included in the current sprint, and

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.120
must be completed by the ‘scrum team’.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:18.840
This may be over a period of 1, 2, 3 or
more weeks but for software development

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.279
projects only, it is typically 2 weeks.

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:25.999
Unlike the ‘product backlog’ items in
the ‘sprint backlog’ can’t be changed

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.449
during the sprint, any items that are not
fully completed in the current sprint,

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.759
will carry over into the next sprint.

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:37.320
Chris: So, what about Epics,
User stories and Features?

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:42.970
All three of these terms are integral to
each other, so let’s look at Epics first.

00:01:43.809 --> 00:01:48.620
Epics are large tasks that may need
several sprints to complete and

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:50.750
will contain the ‘what’s and why’.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.090
For example, you could be asked to create
a shopping cart for an online store.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:59.210
If you remember back to our first video
in session 2 when we talked about the

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:04.460
process of ‘requirements elicitation’, you
will begin with gathering the high-level

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:09.660
requirements for the project [Epics] which
will then be broken down and refined into

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.220
more detailed requirements [User Stories].

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:16.750
There will be several elements to
this, that can then be broken down

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:19.220
into smaller chunks called stories.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.319
Leah: So, what are Stories?

00:02:21.889 --> 00:02:26.220
Stories are small chunks of work written
in non-technical language and will give

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.680
a simple overview of what is expected.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:33.660
Stories should be a complete narrative,
and provide a tangible user benefit

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:37.930
end to end, whilst also small
enough to complete in one sprint.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.039
Looking at our previous
example, the online shopping

00:02:42.039 --> 00:02:43.920
cart is classed as an ‘Epic’.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:47.910
A ‘User Story’ relating to this could
be selecting the payment method.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:52.090
As you can see this is a ‘subtask’
of the creation of the shopping cart.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.609
Chris: And Features what are these?

00:02:55.389 --> 00:02:58.449
If we look at the shopping cart
again, we have identified one of

00:02:58.449 --> 00:03:02.260
the User Stories as the customer
wanting to select payment options.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.970
A ‘feature’ in this instance describes
a specific solution to this requirement.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:13.010
So, in our example a ‘feature’
could be a drop-down selector that

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:14.380
offers different payment options.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:18.290
Another Feature may be another
drop down option for selecting

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:19.610
different postal rates.

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:23.049
Leah: So, what does the
term Themes relate to?

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:27.709
So far, we have talked about
Backlogs, Epics, User Stories

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.500
and features but what is a Theme?

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.249
When working on a project there will
be Epics and or User Stories that

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:39.220
share common characteristics, these
can be grouped together as a Theme.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:43.900
In the examples we have been using
this ‘theme’ would relate to E-commerce

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:46.070
functionality and personalisation.

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:50.190
Chris: As you can see there are many
terms that are used when working

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:54.109
on ‘Agile’ projects and it can take
some time to fully understand them.

00:03:55.189 --> 00:03:58.780
Don’t worry, as you become more
comfortable with Agile development,

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:02.360
understanding, and using these terms
will become very familiar to you.

